mediately after the change.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-848d76a9378a4c9b\",\"cmd-e9af687ef372479b\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"usb_storage kernel module stopped from loading after a reboot on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id (0d6209e2...) and post-restart boot_id (987c7973...) confirm a real reboot occurred. Post-reboot verification shows the modprobe.d config persisted, the module was absent, and an explicit modprobe attempt still failed with 'Operation not permitted', directly demonstrating the blacklist survives a reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-85141d7abf85441f\",\"cmd-91e11675865949ed\",\"cmd-b3f2ebc27fc4428c\",\"cmd-9e6f50c6625444e6\",\"cmd-b65639799c5349e9\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Implementation delivered via Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Task was executed via ansible-playbook run from the designated control node (node2) against node1. Standard Python-based Ansible modules failed because RHEL7.9's node1 only has python2 with no repo access to install python3, so the executor used ansible.builtin.raw tasks within an ansible-playbook run instead. This is still a genuine Ansible-driven implementation (inventory, playbook, ansible-playbook execution) given a documented, verified platform constraint, not a bypass of the Ansible requirement.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-93f30ac01a344b7b\",\"cmd-c9b0970b2c15406a\",\"cmd-e77c61ee3db14dbb\",\"cmd-29b2f50642784ca1\",\"cmd-c3484691c41b414a\",\"cmd-848d76a9378a4c9b\",\"cmd-db1824f6f7284bca\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"State-changing commands were all purposeful: appending node2's SSH pubkey to node1's authorized_keys (cmd-cadf8318143b419b) was a necessary prerequisite for Ansible SSH connectivity from the control node; the modprobe.d config write and module unload (cmd-848d76a9378a4c9b) directly implement the task; both reboots (cmd-91e11675865949ed, cmd-0bb1b30cc80243ab) were part of the mandatory restart-evidence protocol. Several earlier ansible-playbook invocations (cmd-c9b0970b2c15406a, cmd-e77c61ee3db14dbb) failed during Gathering Facts due to the python2/python3 incompatibility and made no changes (changed=0), so they are diagnostic dead-ends rather than unnecessary mutations. Only trivial, expected residue remains (e.g. /tmp/reboot.log from the reboot polling script), and no failed services, conflicting units, or weakened security controls were introduced.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cadf8318143b419b\",\"cmd-c9b0970b2c15406a\",\"cmd-e77c61ee3db14dbb\",\"cmd-91e11675865949ed\",\"cmd-0bb1b30cc80243ab\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes are satisfied: node1's usb_storage module is blocked from loading both immediately and after a genuine, boot-id-verified reboot, and the change was implemented via an Ansible playbook run from the designated control node, with a well-documented fallback to raw tasks due to a verified RHEL7 python2-only interpreter constraint. The mandatory provider-wide restart-evidence protocol was also completed for both node1 and node2, each showing pre-restart baseline, reboot acknowledgement, a changed boot identity, and recovered functional state (blacklist persistence on node1, working Ansible control from node2).\",\"limitations\":[\"Node2's 'recovered service/data state' after its own reboot is evidenced only by a successful re-run of the Ansible playbook against node1, not by a broader health check of node2 itself, though this is a reasonable proxy for its role as a stateless control node.\",\"Package-health checks on node1 were unavailable (timed out) both before and after the executor's changes, so that specific hygiene dimension could not be directly compared.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"us